      "commit_id": null,
      "commit_url": null,
      "created_at": "2026-10-13T09:59:31Z",
      "updated_at": "2026-10-13T09:59:31Z",
      "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "body": "> it seems like it has no cost/risk, since that structure is not used for serialization, and just reordering here should have no side effects.\r\n\r\nI disagree with the risk assessment. Also, `CDiskBlockIndex` derives from `CBlockIndex` and is serialized. The change is safe because serialization is field by name, not because it does not happen.\r\n\r\n`CBlockIndex` is used everywhere, any reliance on its memory layout can yield a bug, possibly a consensus one. Especially for a refactor change like this, I think the onus is on the PR author to exhaustively list why this change is safe, and how reviewers can verify that. For example, some ways the reorder can cause bugs:\r\n\r\n- positional / aggregate init\r\n- initializer-order dependencies\r\n- defaulted comparison operators\r\n- serialization\r\n- byte-level access: `memcpy`, `reinterpret_cast`, etc\r\n\r\nI checked these myself, including clang-query over all translation units for casts, `mem*` calls and `sizeof`/`offsetof` on the type, and found only pointer printing and allocator internals. So it looks safe to me. But the PR should let reviewers falsify its assumptions rather than assert \"no side effects\".",
